<<<
3. Our)(his. Which of us would wish to be ill in our kitchen, especially when it is also the family livingroom?
===
**3.** *Our)(his.* *Which of us would wish to be ill in* our *kitchen, especially when it is also the family living-room?*
>>>

<<<
If a possessive adjective were necessary, his & not our would be the right one, or, at greater length, his or her.
===
If a possessive adjective were necessary, *his* & not *our* would be the right one, or, at greater length, *his or her*.
>>>

<<<
People of weak grammatical digestions, unable to stomach his, should find means of doing without the possessive; why not simply the kitchen, here?
===
People of weak grammatical digestions, unable to stomach *his*, should find means of doing without the possessive; why not simply *the* kitchen, here?
>>>

<<<
But many of them, who prefer even the repulsive THEIR to the right forms, are naturally delighted when of us gives them a chance of the less repulsive but at least slovenly our.
===
But many of them, who prefer even the repulsive ^^THEIR^^ to the right forms, are naturally delighted when *of us* gives them a chance of the less repulsive but at least slovenly *our*.
>>>

<<<
It is undeniable that which of us is a phrase denoting a singular,
===
It is undeniable that *which of us* is a phrase denoting a singular,
>>>

<<<
-OUR &-OR. The American abolition of -our in such words as honour & favour has probably retarded
===
**-OUR & -OR.** The American abolition of *-our* in such words as *honour* & *favour* has probably retarded
>>>

<<<
the sight of an -or. ‘ Yankee’ we say, & congratulate ourselves on spelling _ like gentlemen; we wisely decline | to regard it as a matter for argument;
===
the sight of an *-or*. 'Yankee' we say, & congratulate ourselves on spelling like gentlemen; we wisely decline to regard it as a matter for argument;
>>>

<<<
the English way cannot but be better than the American way; _ that is enough.
===
the English way cannot but be better than the American way; that is enough.
>>>

<<<
Those who are willing to put national prejudice ` aside & examine the facts quickly | realize, first, that the British -our words are much fewer in proportion to the -or words than they supposed,
===
Those who are willing to put national prejudice aside & examine the facts quickly realize, first, that the British *-our* words are much fewer in proportion to the *-or* words than they supposed,
>>>

<<<
By the | side of favour there is horror, beside ardour pallor, beside odour tremor, & so forth.
===
By the side of *favour* there is *horror*, beside *ardour pallor*, beside *odour tremor*, & so forth.
>>>

<<<
Of agent-nouns saviour (with its echo paviour) is perhaps the only one that now retains -our, governor being the latest to shed its _-u-.
===
Of agent-nouns *saviour* (with its echo *paviour*) is perhaps the only one that now retains *-our*, *governor* being the latest to shed its *-u-*.
>>>

<<<
+ reform of spelling is consented to, reduction of -our to -or will be one of the least disputed items, or, failing general reform, we shall see word after word in -our go the way of governour.
===
+ reform of spelling is consented to, reduction of *-our* to *-or* will be one of the least disputed items, or, failing general reform, we shall see word after word in *-our* go the way of *governour*.
>>>

<<<
it would need a very open mind indeed in an Englishman to accept armor & succor with equanimity.
===
it would need a very open mind indeed in an Englishman to accept *armor* & *succor* with equanimity.
>>>

<<<
of value to the -our spelling is borne out by facts should go to the article -or in the OED for fuller
===
of value to the *-our* spelling is borne out by facts should go to the article *-or* in the OED for fuller
>>>

<<<
-OUR- & -OR-. The contents of this article, with the exception of the next paragraph, are given as opinions only.
===
**-OUR- & -OR-.** The contents of this article, with the exception of the next paragraph, are given as opinions only.
>>>

<<<
Even those nouns that in our usage still end in -our (see -OUR & -OR), aS opposed to the American -or, e.g. clamour, clangour, humour,
===
Even those nouns that in our usage still end in *-our* (see ^^-OUR^^ & ^^-OR^^), as opposed to the American *-or*, e.g. *clamour, clangour, humour,*
>>>

<<<
odour, rigour, valour, vapour, vigour, have adjectives ending in -orous, not -owrous—humorous, vaporous, &C.
===
*odour, rigour, valour, vapour, vigour,* have adjectives ending in *-orous*, not *-ourous*—*humorous, vaporous*, &c.
>>>

<<<
Derivatives in -ist, -ite, & -able, are regarded as formed directly from the English words, & retain the -u- ;
===
Derivatives in *-ist, -ite,* & *-able,* are regarded as formed directly from the English words, & retain the *-u-*;
>>>

<<<
so colourist & humourist, labourite (cf. favourite, of different formation), colourable & honourable.
===
so *colourist* & *humourist*, *labourite* (cf. *favourite*, of different formation), *colourable* & *honourable*.
>>>

<<<
But devivatives in -ation & -ize are best treated, like those in -ous, as formed first in Latin, & therefore spelt without the -u-;
===
But derivatives in *-ation* & *-ize* are best treated, like those in *-ous*, as formed first in Latin, & therefore spelt without the *-u-*;
>>>

<<<
so coloration, invigoration, vaporize, & deodorize.
===
so *coloration, invigoration, vaporize,* & *deodorize*.
>>>

